Make `executable-static` mean fully static, on every compiler.

`executable-static: True` reaches Cabal as `withFullyStaticExe`, and
`modules/cabal-project.nix` sets

    package *
      executable-static: True

for every musl host, with the stated intent that "every executable should
be statically linked" -- matching what haskell.nix's v1 builder does for
musl, which is `--ghc-option=-optl=-static`.

The stable-haskell Cabal fork turned it into GHC's `-static-external`
instead.  That is a *different* fork flag, and its own documentation says
so (`docs/users_guide/phases.rst`):

    Since `-static-external` is not meant for fully static linking [...]
    this option allows to exclude certain libraries.  By default, these
    are: `c`, `m`, `rt`, `dl`, `pthread`, `stdc++`, `c++`, `c++abi`,
    `atomic`.

    You can combine this with `-static` to produce binaries that are only
    dynamically linked against e.g. libc.

So on ghc914-sh every musl executable came out linked against a *shared*
libc, which breaks two things:

  * `x86_64-linux.unstable.ghc914-sh.musl32.iserv-proxy-interpreter` (and
    the ~40 musl32 jobs that depend on it) fails to link.  iserv-proxy's
    `cbits/symbols.i386-musl.c` takes the address of `__aio_close` so the
    RTS linker can resolve it in objects loaded at runtime.  musl defines
    that symbol `hidden`, so it is present in `libc.a` but is NOT exported
    from `libc.so`:

        ld.bfd: cbits/symbols.i386-musl.o:(.data.rel+0x1134):
          undefined reference to `__aio_close'

    A plain `-static` link of the same object resolves it from `libc.a`,
    which is why mainline ghc9103/ghc9124 musl32 pass and this job has
    never once succeeded in 18 builds.

  * `x86_64-linux.unstable.ghc914-sh.musl64.tests.cabal-simple.run` runs
    the executable fine and then fails its "checking that executable is
    statically linked" assertion, for the same reason.

The fork does have a flag that means what `withFullyStaticExe` says --
`-fully-static`: "Link all libraries statically [...] This includes
external libraries, Haskell libraries, as well as libc.  Musl is commonly
used to provide a static libc."  Use that.

Upstream GHC has neither flag and rejects the whole command line, and
haskell.nix builds every v2 slice with this one cabal-install whatever the
compiler is:

    aarch64-unknown-linux-musl-ghc: unrecognised flag: -static-external
    Usage: For basic information, try the `--help' option.
    <no location info>: error:
        `cc' failed in phase `Linker'. (Exit code: 1)

(That is `x86_64-linux.unstable.ghc9124.aarch64-multiplatform-musl.tests.check-datadir.run-v2`
and every other musl v2 slice that links an executable.)

Ask the compiler instead of assuming.  The fork brands its `ghc --info`
with `("Edition","Stable Haskell")` and upstream emits no such field, so
that is the discriminator; haskell.nix already relies on it in
`lib/dummy-ghc.nix`, and `tests.dummy-ghc-info` keeps the two in step.

Compilers without it fall back to upstream Cabal's spelling of the same
request, `ghcOptLinkOptions = ["-static"]` (i.e. `-optl-static`) -- which
is also what the v1 builder passes for musl, so all three now agree on
what `executable-static` means.

--- a/src/Distribution/Simple/GHC/Build/Link.hs	2026-08-24 12:42:38
+++ b/src/Distribution/Simple/GHC/Build/Link.hs	2026-08-24 12:42:52
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 import Control.Monad
 import Control.Monad.IO.Class
 import qualified Data.ByteString.Lazy.Char8 as BS
+import qualified Data.Map as Map
 import qualified Data.Set as Set
 import Distribution.Backpack
 import Distribution.Compat.Binary (encode)
@@ -114,6 +115,16 @@
         | src <- extraSources
         ]
 
+      -- `withFullyStaticExe` is `executable-static: True`, and it means what
+      -- it says.  The fork's flag for that is `-fully-static`; the fork's
+      -- `-static-external` is a DIFFERENT thing which deliberately leaves
+      -- `c`, `m`, `rt`, `dl`, `pthread` &c. dynamic.  Upstream GHC has
+      -- neither and rejects the command line, so ask: the fork brands its
+      -- `ghc --info` with `Edition`, and upstream emits no such field.
+      ghcHasFullyStatic =
+        Map.lookup "Edition" (compilerProperties (compiler lbi))
+          == Just "Stable Haskell"
+
       -- TODO: Shouldn't we use withStaticLib for libraries and something else
       -- for foreign libs in the three cases where we use `withFullyStaticExe` below?
       linkerOpts rpaths =
@@ -121,14 +132,21 @@
           { ghcOptExtraDefault =
-              [ "-static-external"
+              [ "-fully-static"
               | withFullyStaticExe lbi
+              , ghcHasFullyStatic
               ]
           , ghcOptLinkOptions =
-              -- Pass extra `ld-options` given
-              -- through to GHC's linker.
-              maybe
-                []
-                programOverrideArgs
-                (lookupProgram ldProgram (withPrograms lbi))
+              -- Upstream's spelling of the same request, for compilers
+              -- that have neither fork flag.
+              [ "-static"
+              | withFullyStaticExe lbi
+              , not ghcHasFullyStatic
+              ]
+                -- Pass extra `ld-options` given
+                -- through to GHC's linker.
+                ++ maybe
+                  []
+                  programOverrideArgs
+                  (lookupProgram ldProgram (withPrograms lbi))
           , ghcOptLinkLibs =
               if withFullyStaticExe lbi
                 then extraLibsStatic bi
